// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// Expected: c1a0a0 — an authorization code redeemed with and without proof
// key binding. The code travels back to the client on a redirect that nobody
// authenticates, so it is a bearer credential: whoever holds it can redeem it,
// and the attacker holds it. That is the c1.
//
// Both redemptions nevertheless authenticate, and the reason is the point of
// the model. A redemption is HASH(code), and the attacker holding the code can
// compute exactly that — but exactly that is also the only value the client
// would ever send. Producing the value the honest party would have produced is
// a replay, not a forgery: it is non-injective agreement, and Verifpal reports
// authentication attacks only where the recipient accepts something the
// declared sender did not author. A bearer credential the attacker can present
// is a confidentiality failure, and this model says so in the only place where
// it is true.
//
// The proof key half then adds the one thing that would matter if redemptions
// were forgeable. The client picks a verifier, publishes only its hash when it
// asks for authorization, and reveals the verifier when it redeems; the server
// rebuilds the hash and compares. The attacker holding the published hash
// cannot produce a preimage, so it could not steer the check even if there
// were something to steer.
//
// The authorization request is guarded and the redirect is not, which is the
// deployment being modelled rather than a convenience: the request goes
// directly to the server over an authenticated channel, and the redirect is
// the leg that passes through the user's browser and an attacker-controlled
// URL.
//
// What protects the second redemption is not that the verifier is
// confidential — it is revealed a moment later, and the first query says so —
// but that it could not be derived from what was published first. A one-way
// function doing structural work rather than hiding a secret is a shape worth
// having in the corpus.

attacker[active]

principal Client[
	knows public method
	generates verifier
	challenge = HASH(verifier, method)
]

Client -> Server: [challenge]

principal Server[
	generates code
]

Server -> Client: code

principal Client[
	redeem_bare = HASH(code)
]

Client -> Server: redeem_bare, verifier

principal Server[
	knows public method
	_ = ASSERT(HASH(code), redeem_bare)?
	_ = ASSERT(HASH(verifier, method), challenge)?
	_ = HASH(verifier)
]

queries[
	confidentiality? code
	authentication? Client -> Server: redeem_bare
	authentication? Client -> Server: verifier
]
